---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import { latestTag } from "../../lib/version";

const output = `2026-11-05 | advisory | osv-alpine | 8.1 | [high] musl: musl libc before 1.2.7, when an untrusted regular expression is used with regcomp… (CVE-2026-107891) | https://osv.dev/vulnerability/ALPINE-CVE-2026-107891 | affects musl < 1.2.5-r24 (fixed in 1.2.5-r24) [1.2.5-r21 in use: AFFECTED]
2026-09-29 | advisory | osv-npm | 7.5 | [high] brace-expansion: DoS via uncontrolled recursion on nested brace groups… (CVE-2026-102278) | https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7 | affects brace-expansion >= 2.0.0, < 2.1.6 (fixed in 2.1.6) [2.0.2 in use: AFFECTED]
TRUNCATED | osv-alpine | 9 older entries left out`;

const workflow = `      - uses: anchore/sbom-action@v0 # or any tool that writes CycloneDX or SPDX
        with:
          image: ghcr.io/acme/app:latest
          format: cyclonedx-json
          output-file: image.cdx.json
      - uses: yoanbernabeu/stackalarm@v${latestTag()}
        with:
          sbom: image.cdx.json`;
---
<Docs title="Container images" description="Check the system packages of a container image with an SBOM: Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, Wolfi and Chainguard.">
  <p class="lead-in">
    A lockfile lists your libraries, not the OpenSSL, glibc or curl that ship in your image. Give the CLI an SBOM of the image and it looks
    up every package in it, system packages included.
  </p>

  <h2 id="write">Write the SBOM</h2>
  <p>
    An SBOM (software bill of materials) is the list of the packages of an image or a project. stackalarm does not write it: the tools that
    read images already do it well. It reads CycloneDX and SPDX 2, in JSON.
  </p>
  <Code label="trivy" code="trivy image --format cyclonedx --output image.cdx.json ghcr.io/acme/app:latest" />
  <Code label="syft" code="syft ghcr.io/acme/app:latest -o cyclonedx-json=image.cdx.json" />
  <Code label="Docker" code="docker sbom --format cyclonedx-json -o image.cdx.json ghcr.io/acme/app:latest" />
  <p>
    Scan the image you deploy, not only the base image of the <code>FROM</code> line: what the Dockerfile installs on top is part of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="check">Check it</h2>
  <Code code="stackalarm news --sbom image.cdx.json --format summary" />
  <Code label="What comes back" code={output} copy={false} />
  <p>
    Given alone, <code>--sbom</code> lists only the known vulnerabilities of the packages, whatever their date. With tags, a profile or
    packages, they are added to the usual news. The option is repeatable, one file per image.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://osv.dev">OSV.dev</a> is asked about each package in its own ecosystem, and each answer says whether a fixed version
    exists. CVEs listed in CISA KEV or holding a public exploit are marked, as in the rest of the CLI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="covered">What is covered</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Packages</th><th scope="col">Ecosystems</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>System packages</td><td>Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, Wolfi, Chainguard</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Libraries</td><td>npm, Packagist, PyPI, Go, RubyGems, crates.io, Maven, NuGet, Pub, Hex, Swift, GitHub Actions</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Anything else is counted in a warning, never dropped in silence: Red Hat and SUSE packages, binaries copied into the image without a
    package, components without a package URL. A distribution package is looked up under its source package (<code>libssl3</code> is
    <code>openssl</code>), which trivy and syft both record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="read">Read the result</h2>
  <p>An up-to-date Debian or Ubuntu image always carries dozens of open flaws. Three cases matter:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">In the output</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">What to do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>(fixed in …)</code></td><td>The distribution ships a fix, and the image holds an older package.</td><td>Rebuild the image on the current base, or upgrade the package. These come first.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>(no fix yet)</code></td><td>The distribution has not fixed it.</td><td>Nothing to install. Look closer when it is exploited, or in a library your application feeds untrusted input to.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>[unimportant]</code></td><td>Debian judges the flaw is no security issue in its package.</td><td>Nothing. Listed last.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Each ecosystem shows its 30 most pressing entries: fixable first, then by severity. The output says how many were left out. Ubuntu rates
    its records with a priority (<code>[medium]</code>) rather than a score.
  </p>

  <h2 id="watch">In the scheduled watch</h2>
  <p>
    Write the SBOM in a step before the action and pass its path. The watch then reports a package of the image the day a vulnerability is
    published for it.
  </p>
  <Code label=".github/workflows/stackalarm.yml (excerpt)" code={workflow} copy={false} />
  <p>
    On GitLab, set the <code>STACKALARM_SBOM</code> variable to the path of a file an earlier job passed as an artifact. Vulnerabilities of
    packages are advisories: add <code>advisory</code> to <code>fail-on</code> to be woken by them, or keep the default and read them in the
    report of the next alert.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">What is sent</h2>
  <p>
    The names and versions of the packages of the SBOM go to OSV.dev (Google), and nothing else from the file. <code>--sbom</code> cannot be
    combined with <code>--no-osv</code>. See <a href="/docs/privacy/">Privacy and security</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
